[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Jenkins Queue Management, Load Balancing, and Throttle Concurrent Builds"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As Jenkins environments scale, managing the build queue, distributing workloads effectively across agents, and limiting concurrent executions for resource-intensive jobs become critical for maintaining a stable CI/CD pipeline. This tutorial covers advanced queue management, load balancing strategies, and techniques to throttle concurrent builds."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Jenkins Queue Management"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Jenkins build queue holds jobs that are triggered and waiting for an available executor. By default, Jenkins schedules items based on a FIFO (First-In, First-Out) strategy. However, complex pipelines often require prioritizing certain jobs over others."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Queue Inspection: You can view the current queue status directly from the Jenkins dashboard sidebar under the Build Queue widget."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Priority Sorter Plugin: This plugin allows administrators to assign priorities to jobs based on job names, parameters, or folders, ensuring critical hotfix pipelines jump ahead of nightly regression tests."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Load Balancing Across Jenkins Agents"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Load balancing prevents single-agent bottlenecks by distributing builds across multiple nodes. Jenkins provides built-in strategies and plugins to control how builds are assigned to nodes."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Usage Strategies: You can configure nodes to "Utilize this node as much as possible" (default) or "Only build jobs with label-expressions matching this node" to tightly couple specific workloads."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Least Load Plugin: Automatically routes queued builds to the agent with the lowest number of currently executing jobs and assigned workloads."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Throttling Concurrent Builds"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unrestricted parallel execution can exhaust system memory, saturate network bandwidth, or overwhelm external deployment targets (like a shared database). The Throttle Concurrent Builds Plugin provides granular control over how many builds of a specific job can run simultaneously."},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a declarative Pipeline utilizing options to limit concurrent executions at the job level without needing external plugins:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    options {
        buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '10'))
        disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true)
    }
    stages {
        stage('Deploy to Staging') {
            steps {
                echo 'Executing deployment with concurrency control...'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Advanced Throttling with Categories"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need to throttle multiple independent jobs against a shared resource pool (e.g., limiting all database-migration jobs to a maximum of 2 concurrent runs globally), use the Throttle Concurrent Builds plugin configuration."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to Manage Jenkins -> System Configuration -> Throttle Concurrent Builds."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Define a category name, such as "Database-Operations", and set the maximum total concurrent builds to 2."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In your job configuration, check "Throttle Concurrent Builds" and select the "Database-Operations" category."},{"order":20,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effective queue management, strategic agent load balancing, and strict build throttling ensure your Jenkins infrastructure remains resilient, performant, and predictable even under heavy continuous integration loads."}]
